            "Read works in relation to the traditions and conditions that shaped them.",
            "Genre gives readers expectations about form, voice, and convention. A poem, play, novel, memoir, and digital narrative can use different structures to create meaning, and individual works may challenge the conventions they inherit. Historical context can clarify references, institutions, publishing conditions, and debates that were available to an author and audience. Context should deepen close reading, not replace it.\n\n"
            "Literature also participates in arguments about identity, power, class, gender, race, language, and belonging. Compare whose perspectives are centered, whose are excluded, and how narrative choices shape that distribution. Avoid treating a culture as uniform or assuming a text represents every member of a group. Attend to translation, authorship, circulation, and reception. A strong comparative reading identifies both shared patterns and meaningful differences, grounding claims in the works rather than in broad generalizations.",
