            "Political science studies power, authority, institutions, collective action, and governance. Compare constitutional rules with how political systems operate in practice: formal offices, parties, bureaucracies, courts, interest groups, social movements, and media can all shape outcomes. Concepts such as representation, legitimacy, accountability, civil liberties, and rule of law provide tools for analyzing different systems without assuming any institution works exactly as its design promises.\n\n"
            "Policy analysis identifies a problem, the groups affected, the available evidence, implementation choices, costs, and trade-offs. Ask who defines the problem, who has access to decision makers, and how benefits and burdens are distributed. Distinguish policy goals from measurable outcomes and account for unintended effects. Compare cases carefully because political context differs. Use official data alongside independent research, note uncertainty, and separate empirical claims about consequences from normative arguments about what society ought to value.",
