            "Economics studies how individuals and institutions make choices under constraints and how those choices interact. Opportunity cost is the value of the next-best alternative; marginal analysis compares the additional benefit and cost of one more action. Supply and demand models describe how prices and quantities may respond to changing conditions under specified assumptions. Markets can coordinate dispersed decisions, but market outcomes depend on property rights, information, competition, and bargaining power.\n\n"
            "Macroeconomics examines output, employment, inflation, growth, and policy across an economy. Microeconomics studies consumers, firms, incentives, externalities, public goods, and market structure. Models simplify reality and are useful when their assumptions fit the question; they should not be mistaken for neutral descriptions of every situation. Distribution matters as well as total output. Evaluate who gains, who bears costs, and how institutions shape those outcomes. Interpret data carefully, distinguish nominal from real values, and avoid causal conclusions from simple correlation.",
