            "Human geography studies spatial patterns and the processes that produce them. Scale matters: a pattern visible at the neighborhood level may differ at the city, national, or global level. Maps are selective representations whose projections, categories, and boundaries influence what viewers notice. Analyze where people, resources, services, and risks are located, then ask how historical decisions and current institutions created those distributions.\n\n"
