            "From the late fifteenth century, European maritime expansion established sustained connections between the Americas, Africa, Europe, and Asia. These voyages relied on navigational knowledge, ship technologies, commercial ambitions, and state competition. The resulting encounters included trade and cultural exchange, but also conquest, dispossession, forced conversion, and diseases that devastated many Indigenous communities.\n\n"
            "The Columbian Exchange moved plants, animals, people, and pathogens in both directions. American crops such as maize and potatoes affected diets and population patterns in other regions; European empires extracted wealth and organized plantation economies with enslaved African labor, fueling the transatlantic slave trade. Study consequences from multiple perspectives and distinguish voluntary exchange from systems imposed through coercion.",
