            "In the late nineteenth and early twentieth centuries, industrial powers expanded imperial control through conquest, economic pressure, and political intervention. Ideologies used to justify empire often represented colonized peoples through racist hierarchies, while local communities resisted in varied ways. The First World War grew from intersecting rivalries, alliances, militarism, and crises; its aftermath reshaped borders without resolving many conflicts.\n\n"
            "The Second World War was a global conflict marked by mass mobilization, occupation, genocide including the Holocaust, and atomic warfare. After 1945, anti-colonial movements won independence across Asia, Africa, and elsewhere, while the Cold War influenced regional conflicts and development strategies. Decolonization was not a single event: new states faced questions of borders, economic dependence, political representation, and social change. Use evidence from multiple regions to avoid treating global history as only a European or North American story.",
