            "A portfolio is an argument about your practice, not simply a storage folder. Select work that demonstrates range and a developing set of questions; sequence pieces so their relationships are visible. Include concise labels, dates, media, dimensions, and process notes when useful. Photograph physical work with consistent light and scale. A reflective statement should identify methods, influences, and unresolved questions without claiming more than the work demonstrates. Review the portfolio for permissions, privacy, file quality, and accessibility before sharing it publicly.",
